{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And she is a bondwoman.</b> The disputants say that she is not a Jewess, but the correct sense according to the plain meaning is that she is the one mentioned in the section of \"When a man sells his daughter as a bondwoman\" (Exodus 21:7), and she is an Israelite. The reason is that her master designated her for his son or himself to take, and she is not betrothed; and the meaning of \"for she was not freed\" is that she is not a free woman until she is betrothed, according to the law of daughters." "And the word <b>נחרפת.</b> According to the opinion of many, it means designated. In my opinion, the word is from the root חרפה, because she is a bondwoman and a virgin, under another's authority, and not betrothed." "<b>And redeemed.</b> A verbal noun from the [heavy] conjugation, whose verbal noun was not [regularly] formed because of the added heaviness. The meaning is that her father or one of her family did not redeem her before she reached maturity." "<b>Or freed.</b> The holem [is] in place of a hatef-qamatz, for it is one [word meaning] חופש. The meaning is that it was the custom to write her a bill of freedom if the father sold her for a fixed time. And the Masoretes said that half of her is free and half is a bondwoman, and this is the truth." "<b>There shall be an inquiry.</b> Some say its meaning is like concubine, and they said that thus \"your high women\" (Psalm 45:10) [means] daughters of kings, but they did not explain correctly, for the meaning of ביקרותיך is the honored bondwomen, and the yod is elided because of the serving letter [i.e., the suffix] with a hirek, and there was a hirek because of the conjunction of two mobile shevas, one under the serving letter and the second under the first root letter in the feminine — and the tongue has no power to pronounce thus; and so is the rule of all language, and there is no foreign word except the word ורבים in \"and many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake\" (Daniel 12:2), according to the reading of Ben Naftali. Some say בקורת, from the root of \"the priest shall not inspect\" (Leviticus 13:36), meaning that he shall search the matter, and the Masoretes said that [this case] incites lashes with a cowhide strap, and this is true from received tradition, and the word is like an asmakhta. And the reason to mention this section is that it is a wronging of the master of the bondwoman; therefore he shall bring a ram as a guilt-offering. And furthermore, by way of derash, this act of lying [with her] is like kilayim, a free man with a bondwoman."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:11:10.296707278Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}